  "guidebook": {
    "intro": "Swahili has two everyday ways to talk about the past: -me- for what has just happened or is done, and -li- for what happened then.",
    "phrases": [
      { "target": "Umekula?", "base": "Have you eaten?" },
      { "target": "Tayari nimekula.", "base": "I’ve already eaten." },
      { "target": "Bado sijala.", "base": "I haven’t eaten yet." },
      { "target": "Ulifanya nini jana?", "base": "What did you do yesterday?" },
      { "target": "Jana nilienda sokoni.", "base": "Yesterday I went to the market." },
      { "target": "Mwaka jana tulienda Zanzibar.", "base": "Last year we went to Zanzibar." }
    ],
    "tips": [
      {
        "id": "me-perfect",
        "title": "Done: -me-",
        "body": [
          "**-me-** says something is done and still matters now: **nimekula** (I’ve eaten), **nimemaliza** (I’ve finished), **nimechoka** (I’m tired, literally “I have got tired”).",
          "Its negative is **-ja-**: **sijala** (I haven’t eaten), usually with **bado**: **Bado sijala** (not yet)."
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "li-everyone",
        "title": "The past for everyone",
        "body": [
          "The **-li-** past takes the same person prefixes as the present: **nilienda** (I went), **ulienda** (you went), **alienda** (she went), **tulienda** (we went), **mlienda** (you all went), **walienda** (they went).",
          "Short verbs like **-la** (eat) keep **ku-** only in the present: **ninakula** but **nilikula**."
        ],
        "table": [["Who", "Went"], ["mimi", "nilienda"], ["wewe", "ulienda"], ["yeye", "alienda"], ["sisi", "tulienda"], ["wao", "walienda"]],
        "targetColumns": [0, 1]
      }
    ],
    "culture": [
      {
        "title": "Umekula?",
        "body": [
          "“Have you eaten?” is a caring question, and in many homes it’s close to a greeting. If you say **Bado**, expect a plate to appear."
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Stone Town",
        "body": [
          "Zanzibar’s old town, **Mji Mkongwe**, is a UNESCO World Heritage Site: a maze of narrow streets, carved doors and old merchant houses where Swahili, Arab, Persian, Indian and European histories meet."
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
